{
  "dom_id": "step-q60172",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 2x-2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 &  \\\\ 2x-2y=3 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 4x-4y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$",
